// DAY 3 — THE SHIKAR. The knock, breakfast, then the shikar: machans, beaters, and a tiger that never comes. Two of
// three things to look into while the beat goes on: the pugmarks, the place Sudam's "remains" were found, and
// Mr. Sahukar's blaze marks on the sal. Then a peacock, a rifle, and a choice. Evening, the bolt, Night 3.
// Clues: pugmarks, remains-place, blaze-marks.

// The jungle at the machans: the road's set, stopped.
place "shikar" set="jungle-road" light="day" {
    sits "bhima" at="-1.2 -2.5" turn=170
    eye at="0 0" y=1.62 turn=0
}

scene "morning-3" day=3 place="breakfast" first="knock-3" name="Day 3 — The Shikar"

beat "knock-3" scene="morning-3" {
    line "narrator" "Knock. Knock. Knock. Bhima, with tea, and a shikar jacket from the Raja's own wardrobe, which fits you like a tent fits a pole."
    line "raja" "The shikar! Today we find the tiger's widow and her children and we make orphans of them. Ha!" if="suspicion<=4" sound="laugh"
    line "raja" "The shikar. You will stay close to me today, Mr. Thistlewood. The jungle is dangerous for a man who wanders." if="suspicion>=5"
    line "mrs-ghatak" "Lilabati will stay in the jeep. She is allergic to tigers."
    line "mrs-sahukar" "And to beaters. They shout so."
    does resets="slots"
    go "machans"
}

beat "machans" scene="shikar-scene" {
    line "narrator" "The machans are platforms of bamboo lashed high in the sal. Below, two hundred beaters with drums and tins begin the hanka, a wall of noise walking slowly towards you through the jungle."
    line "narrator" "There is time, while the noise comes, to look about. The forest guard, a thin man with a tin badge, waits at the foot of your tree as if he had been told to."
    go "hub-3"
}

scene "shikar-scene" day=3 place="shikar" first="machans"

beat "hub-3" scene="shikar-scene" {
    line "narrator" "The drums are still far off." if="slots<2"
    line "narrator" "The drums are close now. Somewhere ahead, something large is moving away from them very fast." if="slots>=2"
    choose "Ask the forest guard to show you the pugmarks." if="!saw-pugmarks slots<2" go="pugmarks" sets="saw-pugmarks"
    choose "Ask him where Kumar Sudam's remains were found." if="!saw-remains slots<2" go="remains" sets="saw-remains"
    choose "Look at the marks cut on the sal trees." if="!saw-blazes slots<2" go="blazes" sets="saw-blazes"
    choose "Climb up to the machan." if="slots>=2" go="peacock"
}

beat "pugmarks" scene="shikar-scene" {
    does adds="slots"
    line "narrator" "He shows you a stream bank printed with pugmarks: a tigress, broad, and two cubs, small as saucers. And older marks, a month old and crumbling, of the same tigress."
    line "narrator" "\"A mother, sahib. She does not come near men. She has never come near men.\" He says it the way a man says something he has said already, to someone who did not want to hear it." clue="pugmarks"
    go "hub-3"
}

beat "remains" scene="shikar-scene" {
    does adds="slots"
    line "narrator" "A clearing by a nullah, a mile from the palace. Here, he says, they found what was left of Kumar Sudam, in the morning. He looks at the ground, not at you."
    line "narrator" "\"No blood on the leaves, sahib. No drag marks. A tiger is not tidy.\" He spits, carefully, away from the place. \"And the marks on him were all the same depth, like a comb.\"" clue="remains-place" regard=1
    go "hub-3"
}

beat "blazes" scene="shikar-scene" {
    does adds="slots"
    line "narrator" "On the sal trunks along the ride, fresh blazes: a strip of bark cut away and a number painted in red. Hundreds of them, as far as you can see."
    line "sahukar" "Admiring my handwriting, Mr. Thistlewood? Every one a rupee standing up. I had them marked in August. Before the, ah, difficulty. A wolf plans ahead." clue="blaze-marks"
    line "narrator" "He glances round to see that it was appreciated. Only Bhima is there, and Bhima is looking at the trees."
    go "hub-3"
}

beat "peacock" scene="shikar-scene" timer-s=10 timeout="peacock-gone" {
    line "narrator" "The beat breaks out of the trees below you: a sounder of pig, two chital, a jackal. No tiger. And a peacock, heavy and splendid, labouring up into the air right in front of your machan."
    line "raja" "Shoot it! Shoot the peacock! The Englishman's first bird!"
    choose "Shoot the peacock." go="peacock-shot" regard=-1 suspicion=-1 sound="rifle"
    choose "Lower the rifle." go="peacock-spared" regard=1 suspicion=1
}

beat "peacock-shot" scene="shikar-scene" {
    line "narrator" "You miss, by a yard, with great conviction. The peacock lands in a tree and shouts at you. The Raja laughs until he has to sit down." sound="laugh-long"
    go "evening-3"
}

beat "peacock-spared" scene="shikar-scene" {
    line "raja" "Again! Again the Englishman cannot shoot. You would have liked my brother, Mr. Thistlewood. He was a very bad guest at a shikar too."
    go "evening-3"
}

beat "peacock-gone" scene="shikar-scene" {
    line "narrator" "The peacock reaches its tree before you have decided anything. It is the kind of decision you have been making all your life."
    go "evening-3"
}

beat "evening-3" scene="shikar-scene" {
    line "narrator" "No tiger. A boar, which the Raja shoots and the beaters carry home on a pole, and a great deal of whisky in the jeep. The moon comes up gibbous over the palace."
    line "narrator" "Bhima bolts you in. Tonight, past the Durbar Wing, the Dewan's offices: the library, the record room, the billiard room. The Dewan keeps the state's books. Books are where things go to be remembered." sound="bolt-shut"
    ends "night"
}
